---
import CaseStudyLayout from "../../components/CaseStudyLayout.astro";

const title = "Modernizing Tomahawk’s Android App — Derek DiLeo";
const description =
  "How I evolved a real-time Android vehicle status feature from Java and XML into testable, reusable Compose architecture.";
---

<CaseStudyLayout
  title={title}
  description={description}
  backHref="/#approach"
  eyebrow="Android · Kotlin · Jetpack Compose"
  heading="Modernizing Tomahawk’s Android app, one feature at a time."
  intro="I evolved a real-time vehicle status card from imperative Java and XML into a reusable, testable feature built around ViewModels, Kotlin Flows, domain use cases, and Jetpack Compose."
>
  <div slot="meta" class="case-study__meta">
    <span>Role</span>
    <strong>Android engineer · UI architecture owner</strong>
    <span>Scope</span>
    <strong>Incremental modernization</strong>
  </div>

  <div slot="aside">
    <p class="eyebrow">At a glance</p>
    <dl>
      <div><dt>Feature passes</dt><dd>2</dd></div>
      <div><dt>Coordinate formats</dt><dd>5</dd></div>
      <div><dt>Tests added by the team</dt><dd>3,000+</dd></div>
      <div><dt>Shared location use case</dt><dd>2 cards</dd></div>
    </dl>
  </div>

  <p class="eyebrow">The starting point</p>
  <h2>A real-time feature in a legacy UI.</h2>
  <p>
    When I joined the team, the application was exclusively imperative Java.
    Observer patterns were common, with LiveData and Android Observable fields
    carrying state through the UI. Data access often went through static
    singleton calls such as{" "}
    <code>EntityDatabase.instance.getFoo()</code>. The original AgentAvatar
    implementation, which represented a controllable asset, was a monolith: an
    XML layout and Java state holder wired together with Observable fields and
    LiveData.
  </p>
  <p>
    The application is Kinesis, Tomahawk’s flagship Android ground control
    system for uncrewed systems. It gives operators a unified interface for
    vehicle control and situational awareness across air and ground platforms,
    so the status surfaces need to stay accurate as live data changes.
  </p>
  <p>
    My first production use of Jetpack Compose came through automated
    vehicle-pairing wizards. Kinesis launched vendor plugins at startup; each
    plugin began its discovery loop and advertised the pairing Activities it
    supported. Kinesis compiled those advertisements into a list so users could
    choose the appropriate wizard on demand, replacing a CLI-driven pairing
    process with guided automation. That contained workflow gave me a place to
    establish Compose and ViewModel patterns. Once the approach worked, I used
    that foundation to convince the team to move the main application toward
    Jetpack and modern Android development.
  </p>
  <p>
    The card is the operator’s view of the vehicle currently being controlled.
    It combines live position, ground speed, throttle position when available,
    energy state, signal ratio, vehicle identity, and control mode in one
    compact surface. Small changes in the underlying node data can affect
    several pieces of UI at once, so the feature was a useful place to establish
    a better pattern.
  </p>

  <p class="eyebrow">Pass one</p>
  <h2>Modernize the surface without stopping delivery.</h2>
  <p>
    The first pass introduced a ViewModel and Jetpack Compose while preserving
    the feature’s existing behavior. The ViewModel became the boundary between
    the data being observed and the UI state rendered by Compose, replacing the
    direct coupling of the old XML implementation. The Composable obtains that
    ViewModel through Hilt, keeping construction and dependencies outside the UI
    itself.
  </p>
  <p>
    This was an incremental change rather than a rewrite. The application is
    large and still uses a hybrid navigation and UI stack, so features are being
    migrated one piece at a time. New development is Compose and
    ViewModel-first, while existing screens move forward through carefully
    bounded interop.
  </p>

  <p class="eyebrow">Pass two</p>
  <h2>Turn the feature into a pattern.</h2>
  <p>
    About a year later, after deepening my experience with Kotlin, architecture,
    and modern Android practices, I revisited the card. By then, I had read
    Robert C. Martin’s <em>Clean Code</em>,
    <em>Clean Architecture</em>, and <em>The Clean Coder</em>, which gave me a
    sharper vocabulary for the boundaries I wanted to establish. While waiting
    on unrelated review and integration work, I used the available time to
    revisit the feature and prove that it could be better.
  </p>
  <p>
    I moved persistence behind a domain repository and data-layer implementation
    backed by Room, then extracted business logic into domain-layer use cases.
    Following Android’s recommended DAO conventions, one-shot reads and writes
    are <code>suspend</code>, while observable streams return non-suspending <code
      >Flows</code
    >. I also added Kotlin unit tests alongside Jetpack Compose UI tests. The
    data implementation is a practical example of dependency inversion: the
    domain depends on an interface, while Room remains a replaceable
    infrastructure detail behind it. In other words, higher-level code depends
    on abstractions, not concretions.
  </p>
  <p>
    Node data is exposed as a Flow. When the user changes vehicles—or an
    observed property of the current vehicle changes, such as speed or energy
    ratio—the Flow emits and the ViewModel updates only the UI state affected by
    that change. Position formatting is handled by{" "}
    <code>ObserveLocationUseCase(locationId)</code>, which returns the current
    location as a string in the user’s preferred format: DD, DMS, MGRS, DMDM, or
    UTM.
  </p>
  <p>
    The same use case was later reused by UserStatusCard for the operator’s own
    position. The call site supplies a location ID, and the use case emits a new
    formatted location string whenever that location changes or the user’s
    preferred coordinate format changes. The preference persists across restarts
    through{" "}
    <code>DataStore&lt;Preferences&gt;</code>, also abstracted behind a domain
    interface and data-layer implementation.
  </p>

  <p class="eyebrow">The result</p>
  <h2>Safer changes, faster feedback.</h2>
  <p>
    Future changes are easier because new work follows a standard pattern:
    observe the relevant data, map it into explicit, immutable UI state, and
    render that state with Compose. Shared behavior lives in reusable domain use
    cases instead of being duplicated across screens.
  </p>
  <p>
    Regression risk is lower because the feature is covered at both the unit and
    UI levels. In under a year, my team and I grew the Java/Kotlin unit-test
    suite from zero tests to more than 3,000, and that coverage continues to
    grow. Software is never really finished; it evolves as the product,
    platform, and team evolve. Keeping each piece clean, modular, and testable
    lets us modernize architecture, evolve behavior, and refactor safely without
    slowing product delivery.
  </p>

  <p class="eyebrow">What I’d carry into the next migration</p>
  <h2>The next migration would start with the seams.</h2>
  <p>
    If I were starting this work today, I would establish the domain and
    UI-state contracts before migrating the first screen, then use those
    contracts to guide each incremental feature pass. I would also define test
    seams around platform data earlier so new screens could begin with fakes and
    deterministic tests instead of adding them later.
  </p>
</CaseStudyLayout>

<style>
  .case-study {
    padding-top: 10rem;
    padding-bottom: 7rem;
  }
  .back-link {
    display: inline-flex;
    align-items: center;
    gap: 0.5rem;
    color: var(--color-text-muted);
    font-size: 0.85rem;
  }
  .case-study__hero {
    max-width: 60rem;
    margin: 5rem 0 4rem;
  }
  h1 {
    max-width: 11ch;
    margin: 1rem 0 1.75rem;
    font-size: clamp(3.5rem, 9vw, 8rem);
    line-height: 0.92;
    letter-spacing: -0.07em;
  }
  .case-study__intro {
    max-width: 40rem;
    color: var(--color-text-secondary);
    font-size: clamp(1.15rem, 2vw, 1.5rem);
    line-height: 1.65;
  }
  .case-study__meta {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: repeat(4, max-content);
    gap: 0.5rem 1.2rem;
    margin-top: 3rem;
    color: var(--color-text-muted);
    font: 0.68rem/1.3 var(--font-mono);
    text-transform: uppercase;
  }
  .case-study__meta strong {
    color: var(--color-text);
    font-weight: 500;
  }
  .case-study__body {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: minmax(12rem, 0.6fr) minmax(0, 1.4fr);
    gap: clamp(3rem, 10vw, 9rem);
    margin-top: 7rem;
  }
  .case-study__aside {
    position: sticky;
    top: 8rem;
    align-self: start;
  }
  dl {
    margin: 1.5rem 0 0;
  }
  dl div {
    padding: 1rem 0;
    border-top: 1px solid var(--color-line);
  }
  dt {
    color: var(--color-text-muted);
    font: 0.62rem/1.3 var(--font-mono);
    text-transform: uppercase;
  }
  dd {
    margin: 0.35rem 0 0;
    color: var(--color-text);
    font-size: 1.1rem;
  }
  .case-study__prose {
    max-width: 42rem;
  }
  .case-study__prose .eyebrow:not(:first-child) {
    margin-top: 5rem;
  }
  .case-study__prose h2 {
    max-width: 15ch;
    margin: 0.8rem 0 1.5rem;
    font-size: clamp(2rem, 4vw, 3.5rem);
    line-height: 1;
    letter-spacing: -0.055em;
  }
  .case-study__prose p:not(.eyebrow) {
    color: var(--color-text-secondary);
    line-height: 1.85;
  }
  code {
    color: var(--color-text);
    font: 0.9em var(--font-mono);
  }
  @media (max-width: 700px) {
    .case-study {
      padding-top: 8rem;
    }
    .case-study__hero {
      margin-top: 4rem;
    }
    .case-study__meta {
      grid-template-columns: max-content 1fr;
    }
    .case-study__body {
      grid-template-columns: 1fr;
      margin-top: 5rem;
    }
    .case-study__aside {
      position: static;
    }
  }
</style>
